/**

@page page_machines_hamilton Hamilton

\tableofcontents

<img width="20%" src="https://nccadmin.webspace.durham.ac.uk/wp-content/themes/core/assets/img/theme/branding-assets/durham-university-logo.svg" />

Hamilton 8 is Durham's internal supercomputer.
It is a system powered by AMD EPYC processors.
We nevertheless prefer the Intel toolchain on the machine.


Hamilton's default oneapi module is built against a GCC version which is too old and lacks
important features within its standard library.
Therefore, you have to manually load the latest gcc.
This is usually prevented by the system and leads to a conflict (so that you
can't have multiple GCC versions loaded).
We therefore have to disable conflict checks a priori.
Eventually, we end up with

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module purge
module load oneapi/2024.2
export FLAVOUR_NOCONFLICT=1
module load gcc/14.2
module load intelmpi
module load tbb/2022.0

export I_MPI_CXX=icpx
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For the vectorisation, we recognise that we always need

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 -Ofast -ffast-math
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

If you use mpiicpc, the MPI wrapper still refers to icpc even though icpc is
officially deprecated.
Therefore, we manually have to repoint it to icpx.
The other options to do this (via additional script arguments, e.g.) all failed
on this machine.


All builds use CMake. Create a build directory and configure with the Intel
compilers and the machine-specific flags. The build always includes the ExaHyPE 2
extension.

- Single-node runs, i.e. one rank with all cores as OpenMP threads
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-O3;-ffast-math;-mtune=native;-march=native;-fma;-fomit-frame-pointer;-Wno-unknown-attributes;-Wno-gcc-compat"
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

- MPI production runs
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-Ofast;-g;-mtune=native;-march=native;-fma;-fomit-frame-pointer;-Wno-unknown-attributes" \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS="-g"
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

- Performance analysis runs with single-node tracing (VTune / ITT)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load vtune
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-Ofast;-g;-mtune=native;-march=native;-fma;-fomit-frame-pointer;-Wno-unknown-attributes;-I${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/include" \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS="-g;-L${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/lib64;-littnotify"
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

- MPI performance analysis runs (VTune + ITAC)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load vtune
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_CXX_FLAGS="-Ofast;-g;-mtune=native;-march=native;-fma;-fomit-frame-pointer;-Wno-unknown-attributes;-I${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/include;-I${VT_ROOT}/include" \
  -DPEANO_EXTRA_LINK_FLAGS="-g;-L${VT_LIB_DIR};-L${VTUNE_HOME}/vtune/latest/lib64;-lVT;-littnotify"
make -j
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Further to these flags, we @ref page_faq_home "pause and resume the data collection" manually in
ExaHyPE and Swift. This is however something we do in the main() routine of
the respective applications.

*/
